    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter today\u2019s slate with **Yordan Alvarez** providing the clear anchor of the offense; his 84.09 projection against Seth Lugo is the only true elite play on the board. **Alvarez** benefits from a favorable 4.59 xERA matchup and his elite .444 xwOBA, which consistently mitigates any park-related suppression. Joining him as a core building block is **Junior Caminero**, whose 70.76 score against Springs is bolstered by a significant 19% boost in SP skill efficiency. We are also leaning heavily on **Colt Keith** (69.44) in the three-hole; while he faces Erick Fedde, the combination of a favorable platoon split and the run-production potential of batting third makes him a high-floor start relative to the rest of the utility-heavy lower order.\n\nTactical adjustments remain crucial given our emphasis on stolen bases and category scarcity. **Chase Meidroth** is a priority start at 61.73; his projection is elevated by a 10% bonus for the leadoff spot against the southpaw Framber Valdez, and his ability to contribute in speed-heavy categories keeps him ahead of more power-reliant options. Conversely, **Trea Turner** (56.45) presents a moderate risk today; despite his high raw talent, the matchup against Nolan McLean is neutralized by poor park factors and a dip in SP skill performance. While no bench players are currently threatening the starting lineup due to the aforementioned off-days for **Pete Crow-Armstrong**, **Christian Yelich**, **Kazuma Okamoto**, and **Christian Encarnacion-Strand**, we are closely monitoring **Caleb Durbin** (49.09). His proximity to the marginal threshold is concerning, but his speed profile warrants the start over potential depth replacements like **Josh Smith** or **Casey Schmitt** to keep our SB volume competitive in a tight 5x5 race.\n\nAll lineups remain subject to final confirmation, particularly for **Dillon Dingler** and **Carson Benge**, whose mid-order spots leave them vulnerable to late-breaking lineup volatility. **Dillon Dingler** is currently sitting on a razor-thin margin (48.59); if he drops in the order or if the weather shifts, he is the first candidate to be pulled to preserve our daily game caps. Meanwhile, **Hector Rodriguez** remains our lowest-scored starter at 46.06; he is an efficiency liability today due to the L/L platoon disadvantage against Wrobleski, and we are tracking his performance closely alongside reserves like **Lars Nootbaar** and **Pedro Ramirez** to ensure we aren't leaving points on the table during this critical end-of-season stretch.",
